One way to identify differences between two byte arrays and where those differences are is to compare each byte in the two arrays sequentially and create a separate array to track the index of any differences found. Here's an example implementation:

```
public static int[] GetByteDifferences(byte[] byteArray1, byte[] byteArray2)
{
    if (byteArray1 == null || byteArray2 == null)
        throw new ArgumentException("byteArray1 and byteArray2 cannot be null");

    int length = Math.Min(byteArray1.Length, byteArray2.Length);
    int[] differences = new int[length];

    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++)
    {
        if (byteArray1[i] != byteArray2[i])
        {
            differences[count] = i;
            count++;
        }
    }

    if (byteArray1.Length != byteArray2.Length)
    {
        differences[count] = length;
        count++;
    }

    return differences[..count];
}
```

In this implementation, the `GetByteDifferences` method takes two byte arrays as input, and creates an array to track any differences found between the two arrays. The method iterates through the byte arrays sequentially, and if a difference is found between the two arrays at a specific index, then the index of the difference is added to the `differences` array. If the two byte arrays are of different lengths, then the index of the first differing byte after the end of the shorter byte array is also added to the `differences` array. The method then returns the `differences` array, but with the unused portion of the array trimmed using the slice syntax (`[..count]`).

Note that this implementation assumes that the two byte arrays are of equal dimensions or that the differences are located at the end of the longer byte array after the end of the shorter byte array. If this is not the case, additional checks will be needed to ensure that the resulting byte differences are accurate.

