Creating a complete ASP.NET Core application set up with SignalR is a bit too involved to fit into a single response, but I can guide you through the process to set up the basics.

You can follow these steps below to create a new ASP.NET Core app with SignalR using the command line or Visual Studio:

1. **Install .NET Core SDK**: First, ensure you have the .NET Core SDK installed on your computer.

2. **Create New ASP.NET Core Project**: Open a command prompt or terminal and run the following command:

   ```
   dotnet new webapp -o SignalRApp
   ```

   This will create a new ASP.NET Core Razor Pages Application. (Please note that for a more detailed application like MVC, you may choose `mvc` template using `dotnet new mvc` command).

3. **Add SignalR to the Project**: Move to the new project directory and add the SignalR library:

   ```
   cd SignalRApp
   dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR
   ```

4. **Create a SignalR Hub**: Hubs are the core of SignalR. Create a new file named `ChatHub.cs` in the project with this code:

   ```csharp
   using System.Threading.Tasks;
   using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR;

   public class ChatHub : Hub
   {
       public async Task SendMessage(string user, string message)
       {
           await Clients.All.SendAsync("ReceiveMessage", user, message);
       }
   }
   ```

5. **Configure SignalR in Startup.cs**:
   - In the `ConfigureServices` method, add SignalR to the services collection:

     ```csharp
     public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
     {
         services.AddRazorPages();
         services.AddSignalR(); // Add this
     }
     ```

   - In the `Configure` method, map your SignalR hub inside the `UseEndpoints` call:

     ```csharp
     public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
     {
         // Existing code for middleware...

         app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
         {
             endpoints.MapRazorPages();
             endpoints.MapHub<ChatHub>("/chathub"); // Add this
         });
     }
     ```

6. **Add SignalR Client Code to a View**:
   - Inside the `Pages` or `wwwroot` directory (based on whether it’s an MVC or a Razor Pages app), create a new HTML or Razor view for the chat client and include references to SignalR scripts. You will also need to add JavaScript to handle the connect, send, and receive actions. If you're using a Razor Pages application, your `Index.cshtml` could look like this:

     ```html
     @page
     @model IndexModel
     @{
         ViewData["Title"] = "Chat";
     }

     <h1>Chat</h1>

     <div>
         <input type="text" id="userInput" />
         <input type="text" id="messageInput" />
         <button onclick="sendMessage()">Send</button>
     </div>

     <ul id="messagesList"></ul>

     @section Scripts {
         <script src="~/lib/signalr/signalr.js"></script>
         <script>
             const connection = new signalR.HubConnectionBuilder()
                 .withUrl("/chathub")
                 .build();

             connection.on("ReceiveMessage", function (user, message) {
                 const msg = message.replace(/&/g, "&").replace(/</g, "<").replace(/>/g, ">");
                 const encodedMsg = user + " says " + msg;
                 const li = document.createElement("li");
                 li.textContent = encodedMsg;
                 document.getElementById("messagesList").appendChild(li);
             });

             connection.start().catch(err => console.error(err.toString()));

             function sendMessage() {
                 const user = document.getElementById("userInput").value;
                 const message = document.getElementById("messageInput").value;
                 connection.invoke("SendMessage", user, message).catch(err => console.error(err.toString()));
             }
         </script>
     }
     ```

7. **Build and Run the Application**:

   Back in the terminal, you can build and run the application with:

   ```bash
   dotnet build
   dotnet run
   ```

Once your app is running, navigate to `https://localhost:5001` (or the corresponding address indicated in your terminal) in your web browser, and you should be able to use the chat functionality powered by SignalR.

If you are using Visual Studio, you can create a new project through the IDE with the ASP.NET Core Web Application template and then follow steps 3 to 6 by adding the necessary NuGet packages, creating the Hub class, updating the Startup class, and adding the client code in your views. 

To run the application you just hit the `IIS Express` button in the toolbar or press `F5` to run with debugging, or `Ctrl + F5` to run without attaching the debugger. 

