In this version, we replace the recursive calls with a nested loop to generate all the sub-sequences. The outer loop iterates through each index of the sub-sequence, while the inner loops generate sub-sequences that begin at that index.

We start by initializing `subSequences` as a list containing an empty list, which represents the sub-sequence of length 0. We then iterate through each index of the sub-sequence, generating all the sub-sequences of length `i+1`. To do this, we loop over the current sub-sequences in `subSequences` and generate all possible sub-sequences that begin at the last element of the current sub-sequence or at 0 if the current sub-sequence is empty. We store these new sub-sequences in a temporary list, and then replace `subSequences` with this list to generate the sub-sequences for the next index.

Finally, we add all the sub-sequences in `subSequences` to the `result` list and return it.

One way to optimize the non-recursive function and make it more time efficient is to use dynamic programming. We can precompute and store in a table the sub-sequences for all possible lengths up to the desired length, and then use those precomputed sub-sequences to generate the sub-sequences of the desired length.

