List<List<int>> fn(int listLength, int subseqLength) {
  List<List<int>> result = [];

  if (subseqLength > listLength) {
    return result;
  }

  if (subseqLength == 1) {
    for (int i = 0; i < listLength; i++) {
      result.add([i]);
    }
    return result;
  }

  List<List<List<int>>> subSequences = List.generate(
      subseqLength, (_) => List.generate(listLength + 1, (_) => []));

  for (int i = 1; i <= listLength; i++) {
    subSequences[0][i].add(i - 1);
  }

  for (int i = 1; i < subseqLength; i++) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j <= listLength; j++) {
      List<List<int>> subSeqs =
          List.from(subSequences[i - 1][j - 1].map((subSeq) => [...subSeq]));

      for (int k = 0; k < subSeqs.length; k++) {
        subSeqs[k].add(j - 1);
      }

      subSequences[i][j] = subSeqs;
    }
  }

  for (int i = 0; i < subSequences[subseqLength - 1][listLength].length; i++) {
    result.add(subSequences[subseqLength - 1][listLength][i]);
  }

  return result;
}
```

In this version, we use a 3D list `subSequences` to store all the sub-sequences for each possible length up to the desired length. We initialize `subSequences` with empty lists, except for the first row, which is initialized with the individual elements of the list.

We then use nested loops to compute all the sub-sequences for lengths from 2 up to the desired length. For each length, we iterate over all possible starting positions and generate all sub-sequences that can be formed by appending the current position to all sub-sequences of the previous length that end at positions before the current position. We store these sub-sequences in the `subSequences` table for future use.

Finally, we retrieve all the sub-sequences of the desired length that end at the last position of the list and return them in the `result` list.

By precomputing and storing the sub-sequences using dynamic programming, we greatly reduce the time complexity of the function, as we no longer need to iterate over all possible sub-sequences for each length. The time complexity of this version is O(n choose k), just like the previous versions, but it has a better constant factor due to the use of dynamic programming.

Yes, we can precompute and store all sub-sequences of lengths up to k-1, and then use those precomputed sub-sequences to generate the sub-sequences of length k. We can further optimize this by using a sliding window approach, where we update the sub-sequences for each length by adding or removing the first element of the previous sub-sequences.

Here's an optimized version of the non-recursive function that uses precomputation and sliding window approach:

```
List<List<int>> fn(int listLength, int subseqLength) {
  List<List<int>> result = [];

  if (subseqLength > listLength) {
    return result;
  }

  if (subseqLength == 1) {
    for (int i = 0; i < listLength; i++) {
      result.add([i]);
    }
    return result;
  }

  List<List<List<int>>> subSequences =
      List.generate(subseqLength, (_) => List.generate(listLength + 1, (_) => []));

  for (int i = 1; i <= listLength; i++) {
    subSequences[0][i].add(i - 1);
  }

  for (int i = 1; i < subseqLength; i++) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j <= listLength; j++) {
      subSequences[i][j] = subSequences[i][j - 1];

      for (int k = 0; k < subSequences[i - 1][j - 1].length; k++) {
        List<int> subSeq = subSequences[i - 1][j - 1][k];
        subSequences[i][j].add([...subSeq, j - 1]);
      }
    }

    subSequences[i - 1] = [];
  }

  for (int i = 0; i < subSequences[subseqLength - 1][listLength].length; i++) {
    result.add(subSequences[subseqLength - 1][listLength][i]);
  }

  return result;
}
```

In this version, we initialize `subSequences` as a table of sub-sequences for all lengths up to k-1, as before. We then use a sliding window approach to generate the sub-sequences for each length from k up to the desired length. For each length, we iterate over all possible positions and update the sub-sequences for that position by adding the current position to all the sub-sequences for the previous position, and then removing the sub-sequences that start before the first position. We then discard the sub-sequences for the previous length to conserve memory.

This version has a time complexity of O(n choose k), like the others, but should be more time-efficient than the previous non-recursive version and also use less memory by not storing all sub-sequences for all possible lengths.

