In C++, it is generally not recommended to call virtual functions in the constructor of an abstract class, especially in the case of pure virtual functions. This is because the virtual function call will not behave as expected during object creation and can cause various issues such as undefined behavior or accessing uninitialized data.

Instead, it is recommended to defer the virtual function call to the subclass constructors, after the object has been fully constructed. This can be achieved by using a pure virtual function in the abstract class and overriding it in the subclass constructors. The overridden function can then be called after the object has been constructed.

Here is an example of how this can be done:

```c++
#include <iostream>

class AbstractClass {
public:
  AbstractClass() {
    std::cout << "AbstractClass constructor" << std::endl;
  }

  virtual ~AbstractClass() {
    std::cout << "AbstractClass destructor" << std::endl;
  }

  virtual void postConstruction() = 0;
};

class SubClass : public AbstractClass {
public:
  SubClass() {
    std::cout << "SubClass constructor" << std::endl;
    postConstruction();
  }

  ~SubClass() {
    std::cout << "SubClass destructor" << std::endl;
  }

  void postConstruction() override {
    std::cout << "SubClass postConstruction" << std::endl;
  }
};

int main() {
  AbstractClass* obj = new SubClass();
  delete obj;
  return 0;
}
```

In this example, `AbstractClass` is an abstract class that contains a pure virtual function `postConstruction()`. The subclass `SubClass` implements this function and calls it in its constructor after `AbstractClass`’ constructor, ensuring that the object is fully constructed before the virtual function is called.

In the `main()` function, an instance of `SubClass` is created and then properly deleted. When run, this program produces the following output:

```
AbstractClass constructor
SubClass constructor
SubClass postConstruction
SubClass destructor
AbstractClass destructor
```

Note that the `postConstruction()` function is called after the full construction of the object, ensuring that all member variables are initialized and that the virtual function is called properly. Also note that since `AbstractClass` is an abstract class, it cannot be instantiated directly. Instead, an instance of `SubClass` is created and then correctly deleted.

In C++, you cannot directly enforce a derived class to call `postConstruction()` after object creation. However, you can make it mandatory for all subclasses of your `AbstractClass` to implement `postConstruction()` function, which would be called at the end of the sub-class constructor.

To achieve this, you can make `postConstruction()` a pure virtual function in your `AbstractClass`. Each derived class of your `AbstractClass` will need to provide its own implementation of `postConstruction()`, which will be called from its own constructor at the end. This will ensure that `postConstruction()` is always called by each subclass after object creation.

Here is an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>

class AbstractClass {
public:
  AbstractClass() {
    std::cout << "AbstractClass constructor" << std::endl;
  }

  virtual ~AbstractClass() {
    std::cout << "AbstractClass destructor" << std::endl;
  }

  virtual void postConstruction() = 0;
};

class SubClass : public AbstractClass {
public:
  SubClass() {
    std::cout << "SubClass constructor" << std::endl;
    this->postConstruction();
  }

  ~SubClass() {
    std::cout << "SubClass destructor" << std::endl;
  }

  void postConstruction() override {
    std::cout << "SubClass postConstruction" << std::endl;
  }
};

int main() {
  AbstractClass* obj = new SubClass();
  delete obj;
  return 0;
}
```

In this example, `AbstractClass` contains a pure virtual function `postConstruction()`, which needs to be implemented by every derived class. The implementation of `postConstruction()` in the `SubClass` overrides the base implementation, and is called at the end of `SubClass` constructor.

When you create an instance of `SubClass` and run the program, the output will be:

```
AbstractClass constructor
SubClass constructor
SubClass postConstruction
SubClass destructor
AbstractClass destructor
```

This way, you can ensure that `postConstruction()` is always called whenever an object of `AbstractClass` or its derived classes is created.

