### Step 2: Define Sorted LinkedList Class with Lazy Synchronization

Here, we define a LinkedList class implementing the lazy synchronization technique. Lazy synchronization involves marking nodes for deletion rather than physically removing them immediately, hence separating the logical state from the physical state of the structure.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class LazySortedList {
    private Node head;
    private Lock listLock = new ReentrantLock();

    public LazySortedList() {
        head = new Node(Integer.MIN_VALUE); // Sentinel node
        head.next = new Node(Integer.MAX_VALUE); // End sentinel
    }

    private boolean validate(Node pred, Node curr) {
        return !pred.marked && !curr.marked && pred.next == curr;
    }

    public boolean insert(int key) {
        while (true) {
            Node pred = head;
            Node curr = pred.next;
            while (curr.key < key) {
                pred = curr;
                curr = curr.next;
            }
            pred.lock();
            curr.lock();
            try {
                if (validate(pred, curr)) {
                    if (curr.key == key) {
                        return false;
                    } else {
                        Node node = new Node(key);
                        node.next = curr;
                        pred.next = node;
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            } finally {
                pred.unlock();
                curr.unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    // Add similarly for contains and delete methods
}
```

