One possible algorithm is to use a hash map to keep track of the unique subsequences. This can be done by iterating through all the subsequences of length `sub` and adding them to the hash map. If a subsequence already exists in the map, then that means it is not unique and we can skip it. If not, we add it to the map and do something with it (such as printing it).

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n * sub), which is much better than O(n^3 log n) for large values of `sub` (since `sub` is usually much smaller than `seq`).

