Debouncing is a technique used in web development to avoid unnecessary processing and network requests by delaying the execution of a function until a certain amount of time has passed since the last time it was called. In React, we can implement debouncing using the useEffect hook and the useState hook.

Here is an example of debouncing logic using useEffect in React, where the dependency array of useEffect is tempNoteText:

```
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  const [noteText, setNoteText] = useState("");
  const [tempNoteText, setTempNoteText] = useState("");

  useEffect(() => {
    const debouncedSave = setTimeout(() => {
      setNoteText(tempNoteText);
    }, 1000);

    return () => {
      clearTimeout(debouncedSave);
    };
  }, [tempNoteText]);

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    setTempNoteText(event.target.value);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <label>
        Note:
        <textarea value={tempNoteText} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      </label>
      <p>You typed: {noteText}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this example, we have two state variables: noteText and tempNoteText. The user inputs their note in a textarea, and the handleTextChange function updates the tempNoteText state variable on every change.

The useEffect hook is used to debounce the note updates. It creates a debouncedSave function that calls setNoteText(tempNoteText) after a 1000ms delay. This delay can be adjusted to suit your needs.

The dependency array of useEffect is [tempNoteText]. This means that the effect will only be triggered when tempNoteText changes.

Finally, we render the noteText state variable to show the user what they've typed. Note that the displayed value will be delayed by the debounce time.

Overall, this debouncing logic ensures that we don't update the note text too often, which can slow down our app and waste resources.

