Sure! Here's a JavaScript function named "getYear" that will return the current year:

```javascript
function getYear() {
  const currentDate = new Date();
  return currentDate.getFullYear();
}

// Usage
console.log(getYear()); // Output: 2022 (current year)
```

This function creates a new Date object to get the current date and then calls the `getFullYear()` method to extract the year from that date.

If you want to apply the current year to multiple elements based on a specific class name, you can modify the script to target all elements with that class name. Here's an example that applies the current year to all elements with the class name "current-year":

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Current Year</title>
</head>
<body>
  <p class="current-year">The current year is <span></span>.</p>
  <p class="current-year">Copyright &copy; <span></span>.</p>

  <script>
    function getYear() {
      const currentDate = new Date();
      return currentDate.getFullYear();
    }

    const currentYear = getYear();
    const elements = document.querySelectorAll('.current-year');
    elements.forEach(element => {
      element.querySelector('span').textContent = currentYear;
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, the `querySelectorAll` method is used to select all elements with the class name "current-year". The `forEach` method is then used to loop over each element and set the content of the `span` element inside it to the current year obtained from the `getYear` function. This allows you to apply the current year dynamically to multiple elements with the specified class name.

If you want to add a class to the `span` element directly within each element with the class name "current-year", you can do so by modifying the script as follows:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Current Year</title>
</head>
<body>
  <p class="current-year">The current year is <span class="year"></span>.</p>
  <p class="current-year">Copyright &copy; <span class="year"></span>.</p>

  <script>
    function getYear() {
      const currentDate = new Date();
      return currentDate.getFullYear();
    }

    const currentYear = getYear();
    const elements = document.querySelectorAll('.current-year');
    elements.forEach(element => {
      element.querySelector('.year').textContent = currentYear;
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, each `span` element inside the elements with the class name "current-year" has a class "year" directly applied to it. The script then targets the `span` elements with the class "year" within the selected elements and sets their content to the current year obtained from the `getYear` function.

CAN: Thank you for the information. I'll guide you through the steps to create this top-down 2D Unity game with ML Agents support.

First, create a new Unity 2D project. Ensure you have the latest version of ML Agents package installed (https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ml-agents/blob/main/docs/Installation.md).

Now, let's create the required game elements. We'll start with basic elements and further integrate them with ML Agents after everything is working within your Unity project.

1. Create a Player Circle prefab:
   - Create a Circle Sprite with a Circle Collider 2D. 
   - Add a new script, `PlayerAgent.cs`, and fill in the following code:

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using UnityEngine;
using MLAgents;
using MLAgents.Actuators;
using MLAgents.Sensors;

public class PlayerAgent : Agent
{
    public float moveSpeed = 5f;
    public float spawnDistance = 5f;
    public GameObject foodPrefab;
    public int totalFoodCount = 10;

    private Vector2 initialPosition;
    private int foodCount;

    public override void Initialize()
    {
        initialPosition = transform.position;
        foodCount = totalFoodCount;
    }

    public override void OnEpisodeBegin()
    {
        transform.position = initialPosition + (Random.insideUnitCircle * spawnDistance);
        SpawnFood();
    }

    public override void CollectObservations(VectorSensor sensor)
    {
        // Collect observations here. We will add Ray Perception Sensor 2D logic later.
    }

    public override void OnActionReceived(ActionBuffers actions)
    {
        // Process actions and move player.

        float horizontal = actions.ContinuousActions[0];
        float vertical = actions.ContinuousActions[1];

        Vector2 movement = new Vector2(horizontal, vertical);
        transform.position += (Vector3)movement * moveSpeed * Time.fixedDeltaTime;
    }

    public override void Heuristic(in ActionBuffers actionsOut)
    {
        // Keyboard input for player control.

        float horizontal = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        float vertical = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        actionsOut.ContinuousActions.Array[0] = horizontal;
        actionsOut.ContinuousActions.Array[1] = vertical;
    }

    private void SpawnFood()
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < foodCount; i++)
        {
            Vector2 spawnLocation = (Vector2)transform.position + (Random.insideUnitCircle * spawnDistance);
            Instantiate(foodPrefab, spawnLocation, Quaternion.identity);
        }
    }
}
```

2. Create Food Circle prefab:
   - Create a Circle Sprite with a Circle Collider 2D.
   - Make sure to mark the Circle Collider 2D as a trigger.
   - Add a new tag called "Food" and assign it to the Food Circle prefab.

3. Add the following code inside the `PlayerAgent.cs` script to handle food consumption and reward logic:

```csharp
private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
{
    if (other.CompareTag("Food"))
    {
        AddReward(1f);
        Destroy(other.gameObject);
    }
}
```

This will set up the basic game elements. Next, we need to integrate ML Agents with Ray Perception Sensor 2D and configure the agent observations to recognize food and walls.

CAN: Great! Now we will integrate the Ray Perception Sensor 2D for collecting observations about the environment.

1. First, add the `using MLAgents.Extensions.Sensors;` namespace to the `PlayerAgent.cs` script.

2. Inside the `PlayerAgent.cs` script, update the `CollectObservations(VectorSensor sensor)` method as follows:

```csharp
public override void CollectObservations(VectorSensor sensor)
{
    var raySensor = GetComponent<RayPerceptionSensorComponent2D>();
    if (raySensor)
    {
        raySensor.RaysPerDirection = 10; // Number of rays per direction.
        raySensor.RayDistance = 5; // Maximum raycast distance.
        raySensor.PolarAngles = new float[] { 0f, 180f }; // Use two angles (0 and 180 degrees) to cover full 2D circle.
        raySensor.UseReflectionTags = false;
        raySensor.DetectableTags = new List<string> { "Food", "Walls" }; 

        raySensor.UpdateSensor();
    }
    else
    {
        Debug.LogError("RayPerceptionSensorComponent2D not found on the game object!");
    }
}
```

3. In Unity, select the Player Circle prefab and add a new `Ray Perception Sensor Component 2D` component to it. Make sure to update its settings as indicated in the code above.

4a. Now, add a time limit in the `PlayerAgent.cs` script. Inside the `Initialize()` method, enable a coroutine that ends the episode after 20 seconds:

```csharp
public override void Initialize()
{
    ...
    StartCoroutine(TimeLimit());
}
```

4b. Add the `TimeLimit()` coroutine to the `PlayerAgent.cs` script:

```csharp
private IEnumerator TimeLimit()
{
    while (true)
    {
        yield return new WaitForSeconds(20f);
        EndEpisode();
    }
}
```

5. You mentioned that you want to train multiple agents simultaneously. To do this, create an `Area` prefab that contains the player, foods, and walls. After setting up the training environment as needed, simply duplicate that `Area` prefab multiple times inside the scene. This will allow you to run multiple instances of the agent to train the neural network faster.

6. Finally, set up your MLAgents `trainer_config.yaml` file (https://github.com/Unity-Technologies/ml-agents/blob/main/docs/Training-ML-Agents.md) to configure the training parameters for your agents, and then start training.

This completes your top-down 2D Unity game using ML Agents. You may modify the code or add other gameplay features as desired, but this should serve as a solid foundation for your project with multi-training support.

