3. **Ease of Maintenance**: With an interface in place, making changes to the common functionality becomes easier. You would only need to update the interface (and its implementations), rather than making the same change in multiple places. This reduces the risk of introducing bugs when modifications are required.

4. **Future-proofing**: If your game engine expands in the future to include more types of sound objects that share these common methods (e.g., ambient sounds or voiceovers), the interface will make integrating these new types smoother. They would just need to implement the existing interface.

