    virtual void Resume() = 0;
    virtual void Stop() = 0;
    virtual void StopFadeOut(int ms) = 0;

    virtual ~ISoundPlayable() {}
};
```

Then your `SoundEffect` and `Music` classes would declare their implementation of these methods. Remember to unify the interface for methods that have slight differences in parameters between `SoundEffect` and `Music`. Choose the most flexible method signature for the interface, and then implement the specifics inside the concrete class implementations.

This approach leads to a cleaner, more maintainable, and scalable codebase for your game engine.

