Here is an example Java code that uses the JavaFX library to display a stream of fast-moving dots from left to right on the screen. The distance between the dots is adjustable in the code.

```java
import javafx.animation.AnimationTimer;
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.canvas.Canvas;
import javafx.scene.canvas.GraphicsContext;
import javafx.scene.layout.Pane;
import javafx.scene.paint.Color;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class FastMovingDots extends Application {

    private static final int WIDTH = 800;
    private static final int HEIGHT = 400;
    private static final int DOT_SIZE = 10;
    private static final int DOT_SPEED = 5;

    private List<Dot> dots;

    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) {
        Canvas canvas = new Canvas(WIDTH, HEIGHT);
        GraphicsContext gc = canvas.getGraphicsContext2D();

        // Create initial dots
        dots = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            double x = Math.random() * WIDTH;
            double y = Math.random() * HEIGHT;
            dots.add(new Dot(x, y));
        }

        AnimationTimer animationTimer = new AnimationTimer() {
            @Override
            public void handle(long now) {
                // Clear canvas
                gc.clearRect(0, 0, WIDTH, HEIGHT);

                // Update and draw dots
                for (Dot dot : dots) {
                    dot.update();
                    dot.draw(gc);
                }
            }
        };

        Pane root = new Pane(canvas);
        Scene scene = new Scene(root, WIDTH, HEIGHT);

        primaryStage.setTitle("Fast Moving Dots");
        primaryStage.setScene(scene);
        primaryStage.show();

        animationTimer.start();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }

    class Dot {
        private double x;
        private double y;
        private double dx;

        public Dot(double x, double y) {
            this.x = x;
            this.y = y;
            this.dx = DOT_SPEED;
        }

        public void update() {
            // Move the dot from left to right
            x += dx;

            // Teleport the dot to the left side of the screen when it reaches the right side
            if (x > WIDTH) {
                x = -DOT_SIZE;
            }
        }

        public void draw(GraphicsContext gc) {
            gc.setFill(Color.BLACK);
            gc.fillOval(x, y, DOT_SIZE, DOT_SIZE);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, the dots are represented by the `Dot` class that holds the position and speed of each dot. The dots are stored in a `List<Dot>` and are updated and drawn in the `AnimationTimer`'s `handle` method. The `x` coordinate of each dot is incremented by its speed (`dx`), and when a dot reaches the right edge of the screen, it is teleported to the left side of the screen.

You can adjust the distance between the dots by changing the `DOT_SPEED` constant. If you increase the speed, the dots will have a larger distance from each other, and if you decrease the speed, they will be closer together.

