Creating a multi-threaded version of the folder comparison tool in C++ requires leveraging C++'s `<thread>`, `<mutex>` and potentially `<future>` and `<atomic>` parts of the standard library for concurrent execution and synchronization.

Please remember that multithreading file I/O and SHA-1 calculations might not necessarily yield performance improvements due to disk I/O being a bottleneck. Depending on your hardware's capabilities (like if you're using a solid-state drive or a RAID configuration that can handle parallel reads efficiently), you might see some improvements.

Below is an example code snippet showing how you might apply multithreading techniques to our folder comparison task with C++. 

This code uses a thread pool pattern and work queue where multiple threads will take directory pairs to process until there are no more directories to compare. The number of worker threads is set based on the hardware concurrency available.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <queue>
#include <set>
#include <filesystem>
#include <openssl/sha.h>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>
#include <future>

namespace fs = std::filesystem;
using json = nlohmann::json;

std::string hash_file(const fs::path& file_path) {
    unsigned char hash[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH];
    char buf[1024];
    std::ifstream file(file_path.string(), std::ifstream::binary);

    if (!file) {
        std::cerr << "Cannot open file: " << file_path << std::endl;
        return "";
    }

    SHA_CTX sha1;
    SHA1_Init(&sha1);

    while (file.good()) {
        file.read(buf, sizeof(buf));
        SHA1_Update(&sha1, buf, file.gcount());
    }

    SHA1_Final(hash, &sha1);

    std::stringstream ss;
    for (auto& it : hash) {
        ss << std::hex << std::setw(2) << std::setfill('0') << (int)it;
    }

    return ss.str();
}

void compare_directories_worker(std::queue<std::pair<fs::path, fs::path>>& dirs_queue, std::mutex& queue_mutex, json& differ_json, std::mutex& json_mutex, std::atomic<bool>& done) {
    while (true) {
        queue_mutex.lock();
        if (dirs_queue.empty()) {
            queue_mutex.unlock();
            if (done) break; // If work is done and the queue is empty, exit loop
            std::this_thread::yield(); // Queue is empty but other threads might be working
            continue;
        }
        auto [current_dir1, current_dir2] = dirs_queue.front();
        dirs_queue.pop();
        queue_mutex.unlock();

        // ... (directory comparison logic here, similar to single-threaded version)

        // Add subdirectories to the queue
        for (const auto& entry : fs::directory_iterator(current_dir1)) {
            if (fs::is_directory(entry)) {
                auto other_entry = current_dir2 / entry.path().filename();
                if (fs::exists(other_entry)) {
                    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(queue_mutex);
                    dirs_queue.push({entry.path(), other_entry});
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    // ... (input checking and initial setup)

    std::queue<std::pair<fs::path, fs::path>> dirs_queue;
    dirs_queue.push({dir1, dir2});

    std::mutex queue_mutex;
    json differ_json;
    differ_json["differences"] = json::array();
    std::mutex json_mutex;
    std::atomic<bool> done(false);

    // Initialize and start the worker threads
    unsigned int num_threads = std::thread::hardware_concurrency();
    std::vector<std::future<void>> futures;

    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i) {
        // Capture by reference since the mutex and queues will be shared among threads
        futures.emplace_back(std::async(std::launch::async, compare_directories_worker,
                                        std::ref(dirs_queue), std::ref(queue_mutex),
                                        std::ref(differ_json), std::ref(json_mutex), std::ref(done)));
    }

    // Join the threads (wait for all worker functions to complete)
    for (auto& fut : futures) {
        fut.get();
    }

    // Set done to true once all threads have finished processing
    done = true;

    // ... (continue with report generation and finishing up)

    return 0;
}
```

A few important things to note about this multi-threaded implementation:

- Each worker thread consumes directory pairs from a shared queue and processes them. Mutual exclusion (`mutex`) on the work queue is necessary to prevent simultaneous access from multiple threads.
- Once the work is done, or the queue is empty, threads will exit the while loop.
- The variable `done` is an `atomic` flag used to signal to the threads that all work has completed.
- We're using `std::async` and `std::future` to run threads and wait for their completion for simplification. Alternatively, you can directly work with `std::thread`.
- Shared resources like the JSON report (`differ_json`) and the work queue (`dirs_queue`) are protected by mutexes to avoid concurrent modification errors and data races.

Please ensure that your machine's setup and hardware support the concurrency level you are aiming for, as threading can introduce complexity and does not always result in straightforward performance gains, especially when a workload is I/O bound.

