Certainly! You can fetch a JSON file and push its contents into an array using the `fetch` API in the browser JavaScript environment. Below is an example of how you can do this:

```javascript
// Assuming you have an existing array
const myArray = [];

// URL of the JSON file you want to fetch
const jsonFileUrl = 'path/to/your/file.json';

// Fetch the JSON file
fetch(jsonFileUrl)
  .then(response => {
    // Check if the request was successful
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
    }
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(data => {
    // Push the contents of the JSON file into the array
    myArray.push(data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    // If there's an error, log it to the console
    console.error('There has been a problem with your fetch operation:', error);
  });

// Note: The myArray will not be immediately populated after fetch(),
// because fetch is asynchronous. You'll be able to access the updated 
// myArray after the promise resolves.
```

Make sure to replace `'path/to/your/file.json'` with the actual URL to your JSON file. Also, this code does not provide any feedback. If you want to work with the updated array, you need to do so after the promise has been resolved. As mentioned in the note, because the `fetch` function is asynchronous, the `myArray` will not contain the fetched data immediately after the call to `fetch()`. You will need to either use `async/await` or rely on the `.then()` callback to work with the populated array.

