Here are some examples of security and cryptography in Java:

1. Encryption and Decryption:

Java's Cryptography API provides classes and interfaces to encrypt and decrypt data using various cryptographic algorithms such as AES, DES, and RSA. Here's an example of how to encrypt and decrypt data using AES:

```
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Base64;

public class AES {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String plaintext = "Hello World!";
        String key = "ThisIsASecretKey";

        byte[] encrypted = encrypt(plaintext, key);
        System.out.println("Encrypted Text: " + new String(encrypted, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));

        String decrypted = decrypt(encrypted, key);
        System.out.println("Decrypted Text: " + decrypted);
    }

    private static byte[] encrypt(String plaintext, String key) throws Exception {
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/ECB/PKCS5Padding");
        SecretKeySpec aesKey = new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES");
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, aesKey);
        return Base64.getEncoder().encode(cipher.doFinal(plaintext.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));
    }

    private static String decrypt(byte[] encrypted, String key) throws Exception {
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/ECB/PKCS5Padding");
        SecretKeySpec aesKey = new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES");
        cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, aesKey);
        return new String(cipher.doFinal(Base64.getDecoder().decode(encrypted)), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    }

}
```
This code encrypts and decrypts a string using the AES algorithm with a secret key.

2. Hashing:

Java's Security API also provides classes to generate message digests or hashes using various algorithms such as SHA-256, SHA-512, and MD5. Here's an example of how to generate an SHA-256 hash:

```
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public class Hashing {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String plaintext = "Hello World!";
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        byte[] hash = md.digest(plaintext.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        String encoded = bytesToHex(hash);
        System.out.println("SHA-256 Hash: " + encoded);
    }

    private static final char[] HEX_ARRAY = "0123456789ABCDEF".toCharArray();

    private static String bytesToHex(byte[] bytes) {
        char[] hexChars = new char[bytes.length * 2];
        for (int i = 0; i < bytes.length; i++) {
            int v = bytes[i] & 0xFF;
            hexChars[i * 2] = HEX_ARRAY[v >>> 4];
            hexChars[i * 2 + 1] = HEX_ARRAY[v & 0x0F];
        }
        return new String(hexChars);
    }
}
```
This code generates an SHA-256 hash for a plaintext string.

3. Digital Signatures:

Java's Security API also provides classes to generate and verify digital signatures using public key cryptography. Here's an example of how to sign and verify a message using digital signatures:

```
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.*;

public class DigitalSignature {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String message = "Hello World!";
        KeyPairGenerator keyGen = KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("RSA");
        keyGen.initialize(2048);
        KeyPair keyPair = keyGen.generateKeyPair();

        Signature signature = Signature.getInstance("SHA256withRSA");
        signature.initSign(keyPair.getPrivate());
        signature.update(message.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        byte[] digitalSignature = signature.sign();

        signature.initVerify(keyPair.getPublic());
        signature.update(message.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        boolean verified = signature.verify(digitalSignature);

        System.out.println("Message: " + message);
        System.out.println("Verified: " + verified);

    }
}
```
This code generates a digital signature for a message using the RSA algorithm and verifies it using the public key.

In conclusion, Java provides a rich set of APIs and libraries to implement security and cryptography in software applications. Developers can choose from a wide range of cryptographic algorithms to encrypt and decrypt data, generate hashes and digital signatures, and ensure secure communication over the network.

